@mhevery , thank you for the explanation, I made another demo and this may related to the `eventlistener`.  

1. In my reproduce repo, if you changed the `login.component.ts` to 

```
import { Component, OnDestroy, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { Router } from '@angular/router';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-login',
  template: `
    <div>
      Login Component <br />

      <button>click</button>
    </div>
  `
})
export class LoginComponent implements OnDestroy, OnInit {
  constructor(private router: Router) {
    console.log('constructor Login');
  }

  ngOnInit() {
    console.log('ngOninit Login');
    setTimeout(() => {
      this.router.navigate(['/hello']);
    }, 2000);
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    console.log('leave login');
  }
}

```

The difference is I removed the `(click)` handler from `button`, and use `setTimeout` to navigate to hello component in `ngOnInit`, and in this case, there will be no memory leak.

So I assume something wrong with the event handler, so I created a vanilla JS project.
2. https://github.com/JiaLiPassion/dom-memory-leak
- First, click `Test` button.
- Second, click `newButton` button.
Then in the memory snapshot, you can find out that the `Test object` is still there.

The code looks like this.

```
class Test {
  constructor(elem) {
    this.elem = elem;
  }
}

window.onload = function() {
  const btn = document.getElementById('btn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
    const div = document.createElement('div');
    div.innerHTML = 'test div';
    div['rock'] = new Test(div);
    const newBtn = document.createElement('button');
    newBtn.innerText = 'newButton';
    const listener = function eventListener(e) {
      const p = e.target.parentNode;
      // 1. remove button from div will avoid memory leak
      // p.removeChild(e.target);

      // 2. remove listener from button will not avoid memory leak
      // e.target.removeEventListener('click', listener);
      document.body.removeChild(p);
    };
    newBtn.addEventListener('click', listener);
    div.appendChild(newBtn);
    document.body.appendChild(div);
  });
};

```

The logic is very simple.
1. When click `Test`, I will create a `div`, and under the `div`, create a button with a event listener. For easier debug, I attached a object `Test` to `div`.
2. When click the new created button, the event listener will remove the new created div from document.

In this case, the `div` will be leaked in the memory.

- But if I remove the new created button before remove new created div, the memory leak gone.
- If I remove the event listener of the new created button, the memory leak still there.

I will continue to do some other research for this issue.

Thanks

Some additional research results.

1. If `removeChild` happened in the event listener of the removed element, the element still exists in the memory.

```
<html>
  <head> </head>
  <body>
    <label id="btn">Test</label>
    <script>
      class Test {
        constructor() {
        }
      }
      const btn = document.getElementById('btn');
      const removeNewButton = function() {
        const newBtn = document.getElementById('newBtn');
        newBtn.removeEventListener('click', listener);
        newBtn.parentElement.removeChild(newBtn);
      }

      const listener = function eventListener() {
        removeNewButton();
      };

      const testListener = function() {
        const newBtn = document.createElement('button');
        newBtn.setAttribute('id', 'newBtn');
        newBtn.textContent = 'newButton';
        newBtn.rock = new Test();
        newBtn.addEventListener('click', listener);
        document.body.appendChild(newBtn);
      };
      btn.addEventListener('click', testListener);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

2. If `removeChild` happens in another element's event listener, the memory leak will gone. The following code demo this case, we have a `Remove` label to remove the new created button.

```
<html>
  <head> </head>
  <body>
    <label id="btn">Test</label>
    <label id="remove">Remove</label>
    <script>
      class Test {
        constructor() {
        }
      }
      const btn = document.getElementById('btn');
      const removeBtn = document.getElementById('remove');
      const removeNewButton = function() {
        const newBtn = document.getElementById('newBtn');
        newBtn.removeEventListener('click', listener);
        newBtn.parentElement.removeChild(newBtn);
      }

      const listener = function eventListener() {
      };

      const testListener = function() {
        const newBtn = document.createElement('button');
        newBtn.setAttribute('id', 'newBtn');
        newBtn.textContent = 'newButton';
        newBtn.rock = new Test();
        newBtn.addEventListener('click', listener);
        document.body.appendChild(newBtn);
      };
      btn.addEventListener('click', testListener);

      removeBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        removeNewButton();
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>

```

